nodecode-cjk-sanitizer is a rewrite of evanyudis's evanyudis/cjk_sanitizer:

  https://github.com/evanyudis/cjk_sanitizer
  commit 47d2a95c89e827fef6ea0bacbff433c6dc980351
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Evan Yudistira (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's one file (__init__.py) is a regular
expression over four CJK ranges and the Cyrillic block, and a register()
that hooked the harness's transform_llm_output. The rewrite keeps the same
ranges and the same two whitespace collapses, written as a character scan
and a text walk instead of a regex, and puts the filter on the seam the
stream actually crosses: an advice on the function every provider lane
makes its stream parts through, so a fragment is cleaned where it is made
rather than after an answer is whole. The range table, the "only Chinese
and Cyrillic, Korean and Japanese kept" scope, and the no-op-when-nothing-
changed posture are the original's.

What was left out, and why: the plugin's declaration also claimed the
callback skipped stripping when the user's own prompt used those scripts
("language learning"), while its README said the callback always runs and
prompt-based detection is future work; the rewrite does what the code did,
not what the prose promised, and says so in (help :cjk). Nothing else from
the original is missing: it had no settings, no command, no tool, no
network, no dependency.

What the rewrite adds: the settings the original hard-coded (which scripts,
whether punctuation, how many blank lines), /cjk to report, pause, resume
and clear its counters, and the cjk_strip tool for text handed to it.
